window.PREP_DATA = window.PREP_DATA || {};
window.PREP_DATA.javascript = {
  notes: [
    {
      "title": "Types",
      "body": "The problem before\nYou treat an array like a primitive, or you use typeof null and think you have an object. Copy a user and both names still point at the same cart.\n\nWhat this is\nA primitive is one simple value: string, number, boolean, null, undefined, symbol, or bigint. An object is a collection of named fields. Arrays, functions, and dates are objects. typeof returns a short label, but typeof null is \"object\" — an old language bug.\n\nWhat it solves\nYou know whether assignment copies the value or a pointer. You use === unless you truly want conversion. You test null with === null, not typeof.\n\nReal-life example\nA lunchbox number (primitive) vs a shared tiffin tray (object). Copying the number gives you your own. Copying the tray still points at the same food.\n\nUses\nEvery variable you declare. Interviewers ask the seven primitives and why typeof null lies.\n\nWatch out\nSaying arrays are a primitive type. They are objects. NaN is type number."
    },
    {
      "title": "Scope & closures",
      "body": "The problem before\nvar i in a for-loop plus a delayed click: every button prints the last i. Or a helper needs a private total and you put it on window.\n\nWhat this is\nScope is where a name can be used. var is function-scoped. let and const are block-scoped. A closure is an inner function that still reads outer names after the outer function has returned. Those names stay alive because the inner function still needs them.\n\nWhat it solves\nCounters, private data, and callbacks remember the right id. for (let i) makes a fresh binding each round so five buttons print 0..4.\n\nReal-life example\nA locker key. You leave the gym (outer function returns) but the key still opens locker 7. Two members get two lockers.\n\nUses\nmakeCounter, event handlers that close over a todo id, factory functions that hide a store.\n\nWatch out\nThinking n resets every time you call count. It does not. Also: var leaking out of if and for."
    },
    {
      "title": "this",
      "body": "The problem before\nYou pass user.hi as a callback. Later hi() prints undefined. You thought this was chosen where you wrote the function.\n\nWhat this is\nthis is set by the call site. obj.method() binds this to obj. A bare call in strict mode has this as undefined. call, apply, and bind set this on purpose. new sets this to the instance. Arrows close over the surrounding this and cannot be rebound.\n\nWhat it solves\nYou keep methods attached when you pass them as callbacks. You use bind or an arrow wrapper. You write class fields as arrows only if they need this in a click.\n\nReal-life example\n\"Who is speaking?\" depends on who was asked, not on where the speech was written. Hand the speech to someone else and the speaker changes — unless you taped the name on (bind).\n\nUses\nObject methods, class handlers, call/apply/bind in interviews.\n\nWatch out\nAn arrow as an object method that needs this. this is not the object. Use a normal method."
    },
    {
      "title": "Event loop",
      "body": "The problem before\nYou log A, setTimeout 0 for C, and a Promise.then for B. You expected A, C, B because \"zero means now\". The print is A, B, C.\n\nWhat this is\nJavaScript has one main thread. It finishes the call stack, then all microtasks (Promise.then, queueMicrotask), then one macrotask (setTimeout, many DOM events). The browser can paint between turns.\n\nWhat it solves\nYou predict order. You stop blocking the thread with a giant for-loop so the page can paint. You know fetch's then is a microtask after the network macrotask delivers.\n\nReal-life example\nOne cook. Finish the pot on the stove (stack), then the sticky notes on the fridge (micro), then the next ticket on the spike (macro).\n\nUses\nAny \"what prints\" interview. Scheduling work without freezing the UI.\n\nWatch out\nA tight loop of Promise.then that never lets the browser paint. Also: Node nextTick is even more urgent than promises."
    },
    {
      "title": "Promises & async",
      "body": "The problem before\nfetch succeeds with 404 because fetch only fails on network errors. Or you forget catch and the rejection is silent. Or Promise.all dies when one of four APIs fails and you needed the other three.\n\nWhat this is\nA Promise starts pending, then fulfilled or rejected, once. async/await is syntax on top. An async function always returns a Promise. try/catch around await, or .catch. Promise.all fails if one fails. allSettled waits for every outcome. race is first done. any is first success.\n\nWhat it solves\nWaiting work reads top to bottom. You can name the failed path. You pick the combinator that matches the product.\n\nReal-life example\nA pizza ticket: pending, ready, or burnt. You do not flip a ready pizza back to pending. You wait at the counter (await) or you leave a number (then).\n\nUses\nfetch, timers you wrap, loading several APIs together.\n\nWatch out\nNot checking res.ok. A 404 is still a fulfilled fetch. Always handle rejection."
    },
    {
      "title": "Prototypes",
      "body": "The problem before\nYou think each array stores its own map function. Or you add a method on Array.prototype in app code and someone else's for-in loop breaks.\n\nWhat this is\nEvery object can point at a parent (its prototype). Missing fields walk that chain until null. Arrays get map from Array.prototype. class is a clearer way to set up the same chain. Object.create picks the parent.\n\nWhat it solves\nShared behavior without copying methods onto every object. You can explain why [].map exists.\n\nReal-life example\nA family recipe book on the shelf. You did not copy the recipe into every child's notebook. If the page is missing at home, you look at the parent book.\n\nUses\nclass User, Object.create(animal), interview \"prototype chain of an array\".\n\nWatch out\nPatching built-in prototypes in a product. You will collide with libraries and for-in."
    },
    {
      "title": "Arrays & objects",
      "body": "The problem before\nYou map and throw away the result. You spread a user, change copy.stats.xp, and the original user changes too. You JSON.parse(JSON.stringify) a value and functions vanish.\n\nWhat this is\nmap, filter, and reduce build new arrays. Spread copies the top layer. Rest gathers leftovers. A shallow copy still shares nested objects. structuredClone deep-copies most ordinary data. JSON copy drops functions and undefined.\n\nWhat it solves\nYou choose copy depth on purpose. You keep the original list when you map. You stop mutating props by accident.\n\nReal-life example\nPhotocopying the cover of a file (shallow) vs photocopying every page inside (deep). Writing on the inner page of a shallow copy still marks the original file.\n\nUses\nReact state updates, merging options, unique lists with [...new Set(arr)].\n\nWatch out\nforEach when you needed a new list. Spread when you will edit nested fields."
    },
    {
      "title": "DOM & events",
      "body": "The problem before\nYou put a listener on every row. New rows added later have no listener. Or a click on a child does not reach the parent you expected because someone called stopPropagation.\n\nWhat this is\nThe DOM is the browser's tree of the page. querySelector finds a node. addEventListener listens. Most events bubble: target, then parents. Capturing walks down first. Delegation: one listener on a parent, event.target.closest tells you which child.\n\nWhat it solves\nLists that grow still work. You use fewer listeners. preventDefault stops a form reload or a link navigation.\n\nReal-life example\nOne teacher at the classroom door, not a teacher on every desk. A raised hand (the event) says which desk it came from.\n\nUses\nClicks, forms, todo lists, any UI that adds and removes rows.\n\nWatch out\ngetElementById for a class. InnerHTML with user text (XSS). Forgetting to remove a listener on a long-lived page."
    },
    {
      "title": "Modules",
      "body": "The problem before\nTwo script tags dump add into the same global. The second file overwrites the first. Load order is a guess.\n\nWhat this is\nA module is a file that exports names. ES modules use import/export, stay in strict mode, have their own scope, and wait for HTML by default. Bindings stay live. CommonJS uses require and module.exports and loads now. In the browser, type=\"module\".\n\nWhat it solves\nTwo files can both have a helper named add. You import only what you need. The graph of files is explicit.\n\nReal-life example\nA labeled spice tin you pass across the counter, not one shared chalkboard every cook writes on.\n\nUses\nEvery modern app file. Named exports when the API will grow. Default when there is one main thing.\n\nWatch out\nMixing default and named at random. Forgetting type=\"module\" so import is a syntax error in the browser."
    },
    {
      "title": "Memory",
      "body": "The problem before\nA dashboard stays open all day. setInterval pushes into an array forever. Or you remove a node from the page but a closure still holds it. Memory only goes up.\n\nWhat this is\nThe garbage collector frees objects that nothing can reach from roots (stack, globals). A leak is data you do not need but still point at. Forgotten timers, detached DOM, huge caches, and closures holding big data are common causes. You drop the last reference. You do not free by hand.\n\nWhat it solves\nLong-lived pages stay stable. You save timer ids and clear them when the component unmounts.\n\nReal-life example\nA locker you still have the key for cannot be given to someone else. Return the key (drop the reference) and the locker can be reused.\n\nUses\nclearInterval, aborting fetch on unmount, WeakMap for metadata that should die with the object.\n\nWatch out\nA global cache that grows with every user id and never evicts."
    },
    {
      "title": "Equality",
      "body": "The problem before\nYou write if (lives || 3) and a player with 0 lives gets 3. Or you test x === NaN, which is never true.\n\nWhat this is\n== converts types, so 1 == \"1\" and 0 == \"\". === does not convert: 1 === \"1\" is false. Object.is is like === except Object.is(NaN, NaN) is true and Object.is(+0, -0) is false. ?? uses the right side only for null or undefined, so 0 ?? 3 stays 0.\n\nWhat it solves\nYou mean what you write. Scores, counts, and empty strings stay real values.\n\nReal-life example\n\"Is this the same lunch?\" === asks same box and same food type. == will call a empty box and a zero the same because both look 'empty'.\n\nUses\nPrefer === daily. Use ?? for defaults when 0 is legal. Number.isNaN for NaN.\n\nWatch out\nif (x) to test for missing data when x can be 0 or \"\"."
    },
    {
      "title": "Careful coding",
      "body": "The problem before\nA clever one-liner works on the happy list and dies on []. Or you micro-optimize a map before you measured, and nobody can read the function in the interview.\n\nWhat this is\nSay how much work the code does as n grows (big-O in honest words). Check empty lists, null, and huge input. Write clear steps first. Speed tricks come later, only if you measured a real slowdown.\n\nWhat it solves\nReviewers can follow the function. Edge cases are in the story, not only in your head.\n\nReal-life example\nA recipe that names the empty pot and the burnt pot, not only the perfect curry.\n\nUses\nEvery function you will say out loud. Interviews, PRs, and your own notes.\n\nWatch out\nClaiming \"done\" without the failed path. Readable beats clever when both work."
    }
  ],
  questions: [
    { id: 1, level: "beginner", q: "What are the primitive types in JavaScript?", a: "A primitive is one simple value, not a collection of fields. JavaScript has seven primitives: string, number, boolean, null, undefined, symbol, and bigint. Arrays, functions, and dates are objects, not primitives.\n\nWhen you copy a primitive, you copy the value itself. When you copy an object, you usually copy a pointer to the same object. typeof returns a short label for a value, such as \"number\" or \"string\".\n\nIn the code: name is text, age is a number, and ok is true or false. empty is null, which means empty on purpose. missing is declared with let and never given a value, so it is undefined. typeof age prints \"number\".\n\nA common mistake is treating arrays as a primitive type. They are objects.", code: "// simple values (primitives)\nconst name = \"Ada\";      // string = text\nconst age = 21;          // number\nconst ok = true;         // boolean = yes / no\nconst empty = null;      // null = empty on purpose\nlet missing;             // undefined = not set yet\nconsole.log(typeof age); // \"number\"" },
    { id: 2, level: "beginner", q: "What is the difference between let, const, and var?", a: "let, const, and var are three ways to create a name that holds a value. let can be pointed at a new value later. const cannot be pointed at a new value, though an object stored in const can still have its fields changed. var is the old style.\n\nlet and const live only inside the nearest { } block. var lives in the whole function, even outside an if. That is why var can leak and surprise you.\n\nIn the code: old is a var, so it belongs to the whole function demo. score is a let, so you may change it from 10 to 11. name is a const string, so name = \"Bob\" is commented out because it would throw.\n\nUse const by default, let when the name must change, and avoid var in new code.", code: "function demo() { // a reusable step\n  var old = 1;     // lives in the whole function\n  let score = 10;  // lives only in this block\n  const name = \"Ada\"; // cannot do name = \"Bob\"\n  score = 11;      // let can change\n  // name = \"Bob\"; // error\n}" },
    { id: 3, level: "beginner", q: "What is hoisting?", a: "Hoisting means JavaScript sets up declarations before it runs the lines from top to bottom. A function declaration is fully ready at the top of its scope. var exists early, but its value is undefined until the assignment line runs.\n\nlet and const are also hoisted, but they stay in a dead zone until their line runs. Touching them too soon throws a ReferenceError.\n\nIn the code: console.log(a) prints undefined because var a exists, but 5 has not been assigned yet. The let b log is commented out because it would throw. ok() works before the function line because the function declaration was hoisted.\n\nDeclare names before you use them so you do not depend on this behavior.", code: "console.log(a); // undefined (var is hoisted)\nvar a = 5; // a named box\n\n// console.log(b); // error: too early\nlet b = 2; // a named box\n\nok();            // works: function is hoisted\nfunction ok() { // a reusable step\n  return \"hi\"; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 4, level: "beginner", q: "What does === do that == does not?", a: "=== checks both value and type. == tries to convert types so the two sides might match. With ===, the number 1 and the text \"1\" are not equal.\n\n== would call them equal after converting. == also makes odd matches such as 0 == \"\".\n\nIn the code: 1 === \"1\" is false, and 1 == \"1\" is true. 0 == \"\" is true, which is the surprising case. 0 === \"\" is false. age === 18 is true because both sides are the number 18.\n\nPrefer === unless you really want conversion.", code: "console.log(1 === \"1\"); // false: number vs text\nconsole.log(1 == \"1\");  // true: == changes types\nconsole.log(0 == \"\");   // true: surprising!\nconsole.log(0 === \"\");  // false: safer check\nconst age = 18; // a named box\nconsole.log(age === 18); // true, same type and value" },
    { id: 5, level: "beginner", q: "Why is typeof null 'object'?", a: "null is a primitive that means empty on purpose. typeof null still returns \"object\". That is an old bug from the first version of JavaScript, and it was never fully fixed.\n\nDo not use typeof to test for null. Use value === null. typeof {} is also \"object\", so typeof cannot tell null apart from a real object.\n\nIn the code: typeof null prints \"object\". typeof {} prints \"object\" too, for a real object. value is null, and the if uses === null, which is the safe test. It then prints \"empty on purpose\".\n\nUse === null whenever you need to know if a value was cleared on purpose.", code: "console.log(typeof null);     // \"object\" (old bug)\nconsole.log(typeof {});      // \"object\" (real object)\nconst value = null; // a named box\n// the safe test:\nif (value === null) { // pick a path\n  console.log(\"empty on purpose\"); // print to see it\n}" },
    { id: 6, level: "beginner", q: "What is a closure?", a: "A closure is a function that can still read variables from the function that created it, even after that outer function has finished. The inner function keeps those variables alive.\n\nPeople use closures to hide data, make counters, and write callbacks. Each call to the outer function can get its own private variables.\n\nIn the code: makeCounter creates n set to 0, then returns next. next adds 1 to n and returns n. count is that inner function. The first count() prints 1. The second count() prints 2, because n was remembered.\n\nA common mistake is thinking n is reset every time you call count. It is not.", code: "function makeCounter() { // a reusable step\n  let n = 0;              // private number\n  return function next() { // send this back\n    n = n + 1;            // still sees n\n    return n; // send this back\n  };\n}\nconst count = makeCounter(); // a named box\nconsole.log(count());     // 1\nconsole.log(count());     // 2" },
    { id: 7, level: "beginner", q: "What is the difference between null and undefined?", a: "undefined means nobody set this yet. null means you set it to empty on purpose. They are both empty-ish, but they are not the same value.\n\nA missing object field is usually undefined. You assign null when you want to clear something. Check both if you only care that data is missing.\n\nIn the code: city is declared with let and never assigned, so it is undefined. user has name but no age, so user.age is undefined. Then user.age is set to null. The last two logs print undefined and null.\n\nDo not mix them by accident when you test if data exists.", code: "let city;                 // undefined: not set\nconst user = { name: \"Ada\" }; // a named box\nconsole.log(user.age);    // undefined: no such field\nuser.age = null;          // we cleared it on purpose\nconsole.log(city);        // undefined\nconsole.log(user.age);    // null" },
    { id: 8, level: "beginner", q: "How do you copy an object?", a: "Copying an object can mean a shallow copy or a deep copy. A shallow copy copies only the top layer. Nested objects inside still point to the same inner object.\n\nSpread { ...obj } and Object.assign make shallow copies. structuredClone makes a deep copy of most normal data. JSON copy drops functions and undefined.\n\nIn the code: user has name and a nested stats object. copy is a shallow spread. Changing copy.name does not change user.name. Changing copy.stats.xp also changes user.stats, because stats was shared. deep is a structuredClone, so nested data is separate.\n\nUse a deep copy when you will edit nested fields and must leave the original alone.", code: "const user = { name: \"Ada\", stats: { xp: 10 } }; // a named box\nconst copy = { ...user };       // shallow copy\ncopy.name = \"Bob\";              // only copy.name changes\ncopy.stats.xp = 99;             // also changes user.stats!\nconst deep = structuredClone(user); // nested copy too" },
    { id: 9, level: "beginner", q: "What is the spread operator?", a: "Spread is the three dots ... that pour items out of an array or object. You can copy arrays, merge objects, or pass many arguments.\n\nRest is the opposite: it gathers leftover values into an array. Spread is shallow, so nested objects are still shared.\n\nIn the code: a is [1, 2] and b is [...a, 3], so b is [1, 2, 3]. full copies user and adds age. sum uses rest ...nums so every argument lands in one array.\n\nUse spread to copy or combine without editing the original top-level object.", code: "const a = [1, 2]; // a named box\nconst b = [...a, 3];     // copy list and add 3\nconst user = { name: \"Ada\" }; // a named box\nconst full = { ...user, age: 21 }; // copy + extra field\nfunction sum(...nums) {  // rest: gather arguments\n  return nums; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 10, level: "beginner", q: "What is destructuring?", a: "Destructuring unpacks values from an array or object into variables in one line. You can rename fields, set defaults, and nest the pattern.\n\nIt saves repeating user.name. APIs and React props use this a lot.\n\nIn the code: { name, age } pulls those two fields from user. { name: n } pulls name but stores it in n. [first, second] takes \"a\" and \"b\" from a list. The log prints Ada and a.\n\nA common mistake is assuming a missing field throws. It is usually undefined unless you set a default.", code: "const user = { name: \"Ada\", age: 21 }; // a named box\nconst { name, age } = user;     // pull fields out\nconst { name: n } = user;       // rename to n\nconst [first, second] = [\"a\", \"b\"]; // from a list\nconsole.log(name, first); // print to see it" },
    { id: 11, level: "beginner", q: "What is the difference between map and forEach?", a: "forEach runs a function for each item and does not give you a new list. map builds a new list of changed values.\n\nfilter keeps some items. reduce boils a list down to one value. Do not throw away the result of map. Do not expect forEach to return a list.\n\nIn the code: forEach prints 1, then 2, then 3. doubled is nums.map((n) => n * 2), so you get [2, 4, 6]. nums itself stays [1, 2, 3].\n\nUse map when you need a new list. Use forEach only for side effects such as logging.", code: "const nums = [1, 2, 3]; // a named box\nnums.forEach((n) => { // shows the idea\n  console.log(n);          // side effect only\n}); // shows the idea\nconst doubled = nums.map((n) => n * 2); // new list\nconsole.log(doubled);      // [2, 4, 6]" },
    { id: 12, level: "beginner", q: "How do template literals work?", a: "Template literals are strings written with backticks. You drop values into the string with ${ }. They also allow real new lines inside the quotes.\n\nA tagged template is a function that gets the string parts and the values. You will see that in some libraries.\n\nIn the code: name is \"Ada\". msg becomes \"Hello, Ada!\" because ${name} is inserted. poem has a real line break between line one and line two. console.log(msg) prints the greeting.\n\nUse backticks when you need to mix text and variables, or when you need a multi-line string.", code: "const name = \"Ada\"; // a named box\nconst msg = `Hello, ${name}!`; // insert a value\nconst poem = `line one // a named box\nline two`;                      // real new line\nconsole.log(msg); // print to see it" },
    { id: 13, level: "beginner", q: "What is NaN?", a: "NaN means Not a Number. It is the result of a broken number operation, such as Number(\"hello\"). NaN is not equal to itself with ===.\n\nUse Number.isNaN(x) to test. Number.isFinite is often better for user input, because it rejects Infinity as well.\n\nIn the code: bad is Number(\"hello\"), which is NaN. bad === bad is false. Number.isNaN(bad) is true. Number.isFinite(10) is true because 10 is a real finite number.\n\nDo not save a score or price until Number.isFinite says it is a real number.", code: "const bad = Number(\"hello\"); // NaN\nconsole.log(bad === bad);    // false! NaN !== NaN\nconsole.log(Number.isNaN(bad)); // true: correct test\nconsole.log(Number.isFinite(10)); // true: a real number" },
    { id: 14, level: "beginner", q: "What is the DOM?", a: "The DOM is the browser’s tree of the webpage. Every tag becomes a node you can read or change. You can find nodes, change text, add buttons, and listen for clicks.\n\ndocument is the entry point. querySelector finds a node. createElement makes a new node. appendChild puts it on the page.\n\nIn the code: title is the first h1. Its text becomes \"Hello\". A button node is created, given the text \"Click\", and added to document.body. After this runs, the page has a new Click button.\n\nUse the DOM to make a static HTML page respond to the user.", code: "// find one element on the page\nconst title = document.querySelector(\"h1\"); // a named box\ntitle.textContent = \"Hello\";  // change the text\nconst btn = document.createElement(\"button\"); // a named box\nbtn.textContent = \"Click\";    // new node\ndocument.body.appendChild(btn); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 15, level: "beginner", q: "querySelector vs getElementById?", a: "getElementById looks up one id. querySelector uses a CSS selector and returns the first match. querySelectorAll returns every match as a list.\n\nIds should be unique. Classes can appear many times.\n\nIn the code: byId finds the element with id main. first finds the first element with class card. all finds every .card. all.length tells you how many cards there are.\n\nUse getElementById for a unique id. Use querySelector when you think in CSS.", code: "const byId = document.getElementById(\"main\"); // one id\nconst first = document.querySelector(\".card\"); // CSS, first\nconst all = document.querySelectorAll(\".card\"); // all cards\nconsole.log(all.length); // print to see it" },
    { id: 16, level: "intermediate", q: "Explain the JavaScript event loop.", a: "The event loop is how JavaScript does one thing at a time but still handles waiting. The call stack runs your normal code first. When the stack is empty, microtasks such as Promise.then run. Then macrotasks such as setTimeout run.\n\nThat is why a promise callback can run before a timer set to 0 milliseconds. The screen can paint between bigger jobs.\n\nIn the code: \"A\" prints immediately. setTimeout schedules \"C\" as a macrotask. Promise.resolve().then schedules \"B\" as a microtask. The print order is A, then B, then C.\n\nIf print order surprises you, check whether the work is sync, a promise, or a timer.", code: "console.log(\"A\");          // now\nsetTimeout(() => { // run later\n  console.log(\"C\");        // later (timer / macrotask)\n}, 0); // shows the idea\nPromise.resolve().then(() => { // a future result\n  console.log(\"B\");        // soon (microtask)\n}); // shows the idea\n// prints A, then B, then C" },
    { id: 17, level: "intermediate", q: "Microtasks vs macrotasks?", a: "Microtasks are urgent follow-ups, mostly promises and queueMicrotask. Macrotasks are bigger tickets: timers, clicks, and many I/O jobs.\n\nAfter a macrotask, JavaScript usually empties the whole microtask line first. That is how you predict print order.\n\nIn the code: setTimeout logs \"macro\". queueMicrotask logs \"micro\". Promise.then logs \"promise\". \"sync\" logs first because it is ordinary code. Order is sync, then micro, then promise, then macro.\n\nUse this when two delayed logs appear in the wrong order.", code: "setTimeout(() => console.log(\"macro\"), 0); // timer line\nqueueMicrotask(() => console.log(\"micro\")); // urgent line\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"promise\")); // a future result\nconsole.log(\"sync\");  // first\n// order: sync, micro, promise, macro" },
    { id: 18, level: "intermediate", q: "How does this work in JS?", a: "this is “who called me,” not where the function was written. In a method, this is usually the object before the dot. In a normal function, this can be undefined in strict mode.\n\nArrow functions do not get their own this. They borrow the outer one. call, apply, and bind can set this on purpose.\n\nIn the code: user.hi uses this.name, so user.hi() returns \"Ada\". hi is pulled off the object. Calling hi() later would not use user as this, which is why that line is commented.\n\nA common mistake is passing a method as a callback without bind, then wondering why this is wrong.", code: "const user = { // a named box\n  name: \"Ada\",\n  hi() {\n    return this.name;     // this = user\n  }\n};\nconsole.log(user.hi());   // \"Ada\"\nconst hi = user.hi; // a named box\n// hi();                  // this is not user anymore" },
    { id: 19, level: "intermediate", q: "call, apply, and bind?", a: "call runs a function now and lists arguments one by one. apply runs now and takes arguments as a list. bind does not run yet. It returns a new function with this locked in.\n\nYou use these when a callback would otherwise lose the object it belongs to.\n\nIn the code: greet reads this.name and city. greet.call(user, \"Pune\") runs now. greet.apply(user, [\"Pune\"]) does the same with a list. bound is greet locked to user, then bound(\"Delhi\") prints Ada in Delhi.\n\nUse bind for callbacks. Use call or apply when you want to run now.", code: "function greet(city) { // a reusable step\n  return this.name + \" in \" + city; // send this back\n}\nconst user = { name: \"Ada\" }; // a named box\ngreet.call(user, \"Pune\");           // run now\ngreet.apply(user, [\"Pune\"]);        // args as list\nconst bound = greet.bind(user);     // lock this\nconsole.log(bound(\"Delhi\")); // print to see it" },
    { id: 20, level: "intermediate", q: "What is prototypal inheritance?", a: "Prototypal inheritance means an object can borrow fields from a parent object. If a field is missing, JavaScript walks up that chain. class is a nicer way to set up the same chain.\n\nObject.create lets you pick the parent. That is why arrays have map even if you did not add map.\n\nIn the code: animal has eat. dog is created with animal as parent, then gets its own bark. dog.bark() uses the own method. dog.eat() is found on the parent and returns \"yum\".\n\nUse this when you want shared behavior without copying methods onto every object.", code: "const animal = { eat() { return \"yum\"; } }; // send this back\nconst dog = Object.create(animal); // parent = animal\ndog.bark = function () { return \"woof\"; }; // send this back\nconsole.log(dog.bark());  // own method\nconsole.log(dog.eat());   // found on parent" },
    { id: 21, level: "intermediate", q: "What is the prototype chain of an array?", a: "An array first looks at Array.prototype for methods. If it still needs something, it looks at Object.prototype. Then the chain ends at null.\n\nThat is why arrays have map and also toString. Do not add methods on Array.prototype in app code. You can break other people’s loops.\n\nIn the code: list is [\"a\"]. list.map comes from Array.prototype. Object.getPrototypeOf(list) === Array.prototype is true. toString still works. Array.prototype.isPrototypeOf(list) is true.\n\nIf a method is missing, look up the prototype chain before you assume the array is broken.", code: "const list = [\"a\"]; // a named box\nconsole.log(list.map);                 // from Array.prototype\nconsole.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(list) === Array.prototype); // print to see it\nconsole.log(list.toString());          // from Object too\nconsole.log(Array.prototype.isPrototypeOf(list)); // print to see it" },
    { id: 22, level: "intermediate", q: "What are arrow functions not able to do?", a: "Arrow functions are great for short callbacks. They do not get their own this, arguments, super, or new.target. You cannot use new with them like a constructor.\n\nDo not use an arrow as an object method that needs this.\n\nIn the code: user.hi is an arrow, so this is not user. user.ok is a normal method, so user.ok() returns \"Ada\". The console log only calls ok.\n\nIf a method needs this, write a normal method or a function, not an arrow.", code: "const user = { // a named box\n  name: \"Ada\",\n  // arrow would steal outer this — bad for methods\n  hi: () => this.name, // shows the idea\n  ok() { return this.name; }  // normal method\n};\nconsole.log(user.ok());       // \"Ada\"" },
    { id: 23, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a Promise?", a: "A Promise is an object for a value that is not ready yet. It starts pending, then becomes fulfilled or rejected, only once. then, catch, and finally queue what to do next.\n\nnew Promise runs the starter function immediately. You use promises for network calls and any waiting work.\n\nIn the code: pizza is a Promise that calls resolve(\"ready\") right away. then prints \"ready\". catch would print an error if reject had been used.\n\nAlways attach catch or use try/catch so a failure is not silent.", code: "const pizza = new Promise((resolve, reject) => { // a future result\n  resolve(\"ready\");          // success\n}); // shows the idea\npizza\n  .then((food) => console.log(food)) // later\n  .catch((err) => console.log(err)); // print to see it" },
    { id: 24, level: "intermediate", q: "Promise.all vs allSettled vs race vs any?", a: "Promise.all waits for every success and fails if one fails. allSettled waits for everyone and never short-circuits. race uses whoever finishes first, win or lose. any uses the first success, and only fails if all fail.\n\nYou pick among these when you load several APIs together.\n\nIn the code: a and b both resolve to 1 and 2. Promise.all logs [1, 2]. allSettled logs a status object for each. race logs whichever finishes first.\n\nUse all when every result is required. Use allSettled when you want every outcome even if some fail.", code: "const a = Promise.resolve(1); // a future result\nconst b = Promise.resolve(2); // a future result\nPromise.all([a, b]).then(console.log);      // [1, 2]\nPromise.allSettled([a, b]).then(console.log); // status each\nPromise.race([a, b]).then(console.log);     // first done" },
    { id: 25, level: "intermediate", q: "How does async/await work?", a: "async functions always return a Promise. await pauses that function until the promise finishes, not the whole page. Use try/catch around await to catch failures.\n\nThe code still reads top to bottom, which is easier than long .then chains.\n\nIn the code: loadName waits for fetch, then waits for res.json(), then returns data.name. If anything throws, catch returns \"Guest\". fetch talks to the network. json reads the body as data.\n\nA common mistake is forgetting try/catch, so a failed request becomes an unhandled rejection.", code: "async function loadName() { // can wait inside\n  try { // shows the idea\n    const res = await fetch(\"/api/me\"); // wait here\n    const data = await res.json();      // wait again\n    return data.name; // send this back\n  } catch (err) {\n    return \"Guest\";                     // error path\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 26, level: "intermediate", q: "What is event bubbling and capturing?", a: "Capturing walks down from the window to the target. Bubbling walks back up from the target to the window. Listeners default to the bubble trip.\n\nUse capture: true to listen on the way down. That decides who handles a click first.\n\nIn the code: the first listener logs \"bubble on parent\" on the way up. The second listener uses true, so it logs \"capture on parent\" on the way down. Capture runs before the target, then bubble runs after.\n\nIf two handlers fire in a confusing order, check whether one used capture.", code: "parent.addEventListener(\"click\", () => { // when the user acts\n  console.log(\"bubble on parent\");     // way up\n}); // shows the idea\nparent.addEventListener(\"click\", () => { // when the user acts\n  console.log(\"capture on parent\");    // way down\n}, true);                              // capture: true" },
    { id: 27, level: "intermediate", q: "What is event delegation?", a: "Event delegation means one listener on a parent handles many children. When something inside is clicked, event.target tells you which node it was. New children added later still work.\n\nYou use fewer listeners and less memory.\n\nIn the code: the list listens for click. closest(\"button\") finds the button that was clicked, even if the click was on text inside it. If there is no button, the function returns. dataset.id is then logged.\n\nUse this for lists that grow and shrink, instead of a listener on every row.", code: "list.addEventListener(\"click\", (event) => { // when the user acts\n  const btn = event.target.closest(\"button\"); // which child?\n  if (!btn) return;                           // clicked empty space\n  console.log(btn.dataset.id);                // handle it\n}); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 28, level: "intermediate", q: "debounce vs throttle?", a: "Debounce waits until you stop calling a function for a while, then runs it once. Throttle runs at most once every N milliseconds, even if you keep calling.\n\nSearch boxes often debounce. Scroll handlers often throttle.\n\nIn the code: debounce stores a timeout id. Each call clears the last wait and starts a new one. After ms milliseconds of quiet, fn runs with the latest arguments. search waits 300ms after typing.\n\nA common mistake is debouncing the input display itself. Keep the letters instant, and debounce the search request.", code: "function debounce(fn, ms) { // a reusable step\n  let id; // a named box\n  return function (...args) { // send this back\n    clearTimeout(id);              // cancel last wait\n    id = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms); // wait again\n  };\n}\nconst search = debounce((q) => console.log(q), 300); // print to see it" },
    { id: 29, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a pure function?", a: "A pure function always gives the same output for the same inputs. It must not change things outside, such as global variables or the DOM.\n\nmap callbacks should be pure. forEach often is not, because it is used for side effects. Pure functions are easier to test.\n\nIn the code: add only uses a and b, so it is pure. addToTotal changes total outside the function, so it is not pure. total starts at 0 and grows.\n\nUse pure functions for calculations. Keep side effects in a small, obvious place.", code: "function add(a, b) { // a reusable step\n  return a + b;            // pure: only uses inputs\n}\nlet total = 0; // a named box\nfunction addToTotal(n) { // a reusable step\n  total = total + n;       // not pure: changes outside\n}" },
    { id: 30, level: "intermediate", q: "shallow vs deep copy?", a: "A shallow copy clones only the first layer. Inner objects still point to the same memory. A deep copy clones nested values too.\n\nSpread is shallow. structuredClone is deep for most data. If you edit nested state on a shallow copy, you also change the original.\n\nIn the code: a has inner.n set to 1. Changing shallow.inner.n to 2 also changes a.inner. deep is a structuredClone, so setting deep.inner.n to 3 leaves a at 2.\n\nCopy nested objects on purpose when you need two independent trees.", code: "const a = { inner: { n: 1 } }; // a named box\nconst shallow = { ...a }; // a named box\nshallow.inner.n = 2;       // also changes a.inner\nconst deep = structuredClone(a); // a named box\ndeep.inner.n = 3;          // a stays 2" },
    { id: 31, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you handle errors in async code?", a: "Use .catch on a promise, or try/catch around await. fetch only fails on network problems, not on HTTP 404, so check response.ok too.\n\nDo not leave a promise with no handler. Show the user a message instead of failing silently.\n\nIn the code: load awaits fetch(\"/api\"). If res.ok is false, it throws. json is returned on success. catch logs a friendly error. The throw is what turns a 404 into a caught error.\n\nAlways check res.ok. A 404 response is still a completed fetch.", code: "async function load() { // can wait inside\n  try { // shows the idea\n    const res = await fetch(\"/api\"); // ask the network\n    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(\"bad status\"); // pick a path\n    return await res.json(); // pause until ready\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(\"show a friendly error\"); // print to see it\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 32, level: "intermediate", q: "What is optional chaining?", a: "Optional chaining is ?.. It stops and returns undefined if the left side is null or undefined. You do not crash on missing nested fields.\n\nCombine with ?? to pick a default when the value is null or undefined.\n\nIn the code: user has no address, so user?.address?.city is undefined and does not throw. user?.name ?? \"Guest\" becomes \"Ada\". The log prints undefined and Ada.\n\nUse ?. on nested data you do not fully control, such as API objects.", code: "const user = { name: \"Ada\" }; // a named box\nconst city = user?.address?.city; // undefined, no crash\nconst label = user?.name ?? \"Guest\"; // default if nullish\nconsole.log(city, label); // print to see it" },
    { id: 33, level: "intermediate", q: "What is nullish coalescing?", a: "?? uses the right side only if the left is null or undefined. || also treats 0, \"\" and false as missing, which is often wrong.\n\nUse ?? for numbers that can legally be 0, such as lives or scores.\n\nIn the code: lives is 0. lives || 3 becomes 3, which is the bug. lives ?? 3 stays 0. name is null, so name ?? \"Guest\" is \"Guest\".\n\nIf 0 or \"\" is a real answer, use ?? not ||.", code: "const lives = 0; // a named box\nconsole.log(lives || 3);  // 3 — oops, 0 looks empty\nconsole.log(lives ?? 3);  // 0 — correct, zero is real\nconst name = null; // a named box\nconsole.log(name ?? \"Guest\"); // \"Guest\"" },
    { id: 34, level: "intermediate", q: "What are iterables and iterators?", a: "An iterable is something you can loop with for...of. It has a special method named Symbol.iterator. That method returns an iterator with next() giving { value, done }.\n\nArrays, strings, maps, and sets are iterable. You can build custom lists the same way.\n\nIn the code: \"hi\" is looped character by character, so the logs are h then i. it is the iterator from Symbol.iterator. it.next() is { value: \"h\", done: false }.\n\nUse for...of when you want values. Use an iterator when you need to pull items one at a time.", code: "const text = \"hi\";              // strings are iterable\nfor (const ch of text) { // repeat\n  console.log(ch);              // h, then i\n}\nconst it = text[Symbol.iterator](); // a named box\nconsole.log(it.next());         // { value: \"h\", done: false }" },
    { id: 35, level: "intermediate", q: "for...in vs for...of?", a: "for...in walks keys, including inherited ones. for...of walks values of an iterable, such as array items.\n\nDo not use for...in on arrays if you only want 0, 1, 2 in order. You can mix up the name of a field with the field’s value.\n\nIn the code: for...in user logs \"name\", the key. for...of list logs \"a\" then \"b\", the values. The two loops are side by side so you can compare them.\n\nUse for...of for arrays. Use Object.keys or hasOwn when you mean own keys.", code: "const user = { name: \"Ada\" }; // a named box\nfor (const key in user) { // repeat\n  console.log(key);        // \"name\" (the label)\n}\nconst list = [\"a\", \"b\"]; // a named box\nfor (const item of list) { // repeat\n  console.log(item);       // \"a\", \"b\" (the values)\n}" },
    { id: 36, level: "intermediate", q: "Map vs Object?", a: "Objects are great as records with known field names. Map can use any value as a key, even another object. Map tracks size and keeps insertion order for any keys.\n\nUse Map for dictionaries that grow and shrink a lot, or when keys are not strings.\n\nIn the code: map sets \"age\" to 21. objKey is an object used as a key. get(\"age\") reads 21. map.size is how many entries exist.\n\nA common mistake is using a new object as a key later. It will not match the original object key.", code: "const map = new Map(); // a named box\nmap.set(\"age\", 21);          // string key\nconst objKey = { id: 1 }; // a named box\nmap.set(objKey, \"Ada\");      // object as key\nconsole.log(map.get(\"age\")); // print to see it\nconsole.log(map.size); // print to see it" },
    { id: 37, level: "intermediate", q: "Set vs Array for uniqueness?", a: "A Set stores each value only once. Adding and checking is fast on average. [...new Set(arr)] is the usual unique-list trick.\n\nNaN is treated as the same as NaN inside a Set, unlike ===.\n\nIn the code: tags has \"js\" twice. unique is [\"js\", \"css\"]. seen.add(\"Ada\") then seen.has(\"Ada\") is true.\n\nUse a Set for tags, ids, and “did we already see this?” checks.", code: "const tags = [\"js\", \"js\", \"css\"]; // a named box\nconst unique = [...new Set(tags)]; // [\"js\", \"css\"]\nconst seen = new Set(); // a named box\nseen.add(\"Ada\"); // shows the idea\nconsole.log(seen.has(\"Ada\"));      // true" },
    { id: 38, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a generator?", a: "A generator is a function* that can yield a value and pause, then continue later. Calling it returns an iterator, not all values at once.\n\nThat is useful for lazy lists. async/await replaced many old generator async patterns.\n\nIn the code: ids yields 1, then yields 2. g is the iterator. The first next().value is 1. The second is 2.\n\nUse a generator when you want values one at a time without building a huge array first.", code: "function* ids() { // a reusable step\n  yield 1;               // pause, give 1\n  yield 2;               // pause, give 2\n}\nconst g = ids(); // a named box\nconsole.log(g.next().value); // 1\nconsole.log(g.next().value); // 2" },
    { id: 39, level: "intermediate", q: "What is currying?", a: "Currying turns one multi-argument function into a chain. f(a, b, c) becomes f(a)(b)(c). You can fill in some arguments now and the rest later.\n\nClosures make this easy in JavaScript. It is useful for reusable configured helpers.\n\nIn the code: multiply(a) returns a function that waits for b. double is multiply(2), so a is locked to 2. double(5) prints 10.\n\nUse this when you want a specialized function, such as “always multiply by 2.”", code: "function multiply(a) { // a reusable step\n  return function (b) {     // remember a\n    return a * b; // send this back\n  };\n}\nconst double = multiply(2); // lock in 2\nconsole.log(double(5));     // 10" },
    { id: 40, level: "intermediate", q: "What is memoization?", a: "Memoization means remembering answers for inputs you already saw. The next call with the same inputs returns the saved answer.\n\nIt only works well for pure functions. Watch memory if the cache grows forever.\n\nIn the code: memo wraps fn with a Map cache. If x is already in the cache, it returns that. Otherwise it calls fn, stores y, and returns y.\n\nDo not memoize functions that depend on time, random numbers, or outside data.", code: "function memo(fn) { // a reusable step\n  const cache = new Map(); // a named box\n  return function (x) { // send this back\n    if (cache.has(x)) return cache.get(x); // already known\n    const y = fn(x); // a named box\n    cache.set(x, y);                       // save it\n    return y; // send this back\n  };\n}" },
    { id: 41, level: "intermediate", q: "How does JSON.stringify handle special values?", a: "JSON.stringify turns data into text. Functions, undefined, and symbols disappear from objects. In arrays they become null.\n\nDates become text. BigInt throws. Use a replacer function for custom rules.\n\nIn the code: data has n, skip: undefined, and a Date. stringify drops skip from the object. stringify([undefined]) becomes \"[null]\". The Date becomes a string in the JSON.\n\nDo not round-trip data through JSON if you need functions, undefined, or BigInt.", code: "const data = { // a named box\n  n: 1,\n  skip: undefined,          // dropped in objects\n  when: new Date()          // becomes a string\n};\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(data)); // print to see it\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify([undefined])); // \"[null]\"" },
    { id: 42, level: "intermediate", q: "What is the temporal dead zone?", a: "The temporal dead zone is the stretch from the start of a block until the let or const line. The name exists, but touching it throws ReferenceError.\n\nvar would have been undefined instead. Declare first, then use.\n\nIn the code: the block has a commented console.log(x) that would throw. After let x = 1, the log prints 1. x is alive only after that line.\n\nIf you see a ReferenceError on a let, you probably used it above its declaration.", code: "{\n  // console.log(x); // error: temporal dead zone\n  let x = 1;         // now x is alive\n  console.log(x); // print to see it\n}" },
    { id: 43, level: "intermediate", q: "What are modules vs scripts?", a: "Old scripts dump names into one shared global space. ES modules have their own scope, stay in strict mode, and use import/export. Modules wait until the HTML is parsed by default.\n\nThat avoids name clashes and makes load order clearer.\n\nIn the code: math.js exports add. app.js imports { add } from \"./math.js\" and logs add(2, 3), which is 5. Only add is imported, not the whole file’s internals.\n\nUse modules so two files can both have a helper named add without colliding.", code: "// math.js\nexport function add(a, b) {  // share this name\n  return a + b; // send this back\n}\n// app.js\nimport { add } from \"./math.js\"; // take only add\nconsole.log(add(2, 3)); // print to see it" },
    { id: 44, level: "intermediate", q: "named export vs default export?", a: "A file can export many named things. It can also have one default export. Named imports must use the same names unless you rename. Default can be imported with any name.\n\nPrefer named exports when an API will grow.\n\nIn the code: PI is a named export. area is the default export. import area, { PI } brings both in. The log prints PI and the area of radius 2.\n\nDo not mix default and named randomly in one project. Pick a style and stay consistent.", code: "export const PI = 3.14;     // named\nexport default function area(r) { // one default\n  return PI * r * r; // send this back\n}\nimport area, { PI } from \"./math.js\"; // bring in a name\nconsole.log(PI, area(2)); // print to see it" },
    { id: 45, level: "intermediate", q: "What is 'use strict'?", a: "\"use strict\" turns sloppy mistakes into errors. You cannot assign to undeclared variables. this is not secretly the global object.\n\nModules and classes are already strict. Strict mode catches bugs earlier.\n\nIn the code: the file starts with \"use strict\". x = 1 without declaring is commented because it would throw. const x = 1 is ok, and demo returns x.\n\nIf you are in a module, you already have strict mode even without the string.", code: "\"use strict\"; // shows the idea\nfunction demo() { // a reusable step\n  // x = 1;           // error: x was never declared\n  const x = 1;        // ok\n  return x; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 46, level: "advanced", q: "Explain hidden classes and inline caches (V8).", a: "A JavaScript engine can learn a layout for objects that have the same fields in the same order. If you always add name then age, it can cache where those fields live.\n\nIf you add fields in random order, the layout keeps changing and lookups get slower. That cost shows up in hot loops, not in tiny scripts.\n\nIn the code: makeUser always returns { name, age } in that order. a and b are two users built the same way. The log prints Ada and 22.\n\nKeep field order consistent when you create many similar objects in a tight loop.", code: "// same field order: stable shape (faster)\nfunction makeUser(name, age) { // a reusable step\n  return { name, age };     // always name, then age\n}\nconst a = makeUser(\"Ada\", 21); // a named box\nconst b = makeUser(\"Bob\", 22); // a named box\nconsole.log(a.name, b.age); // print to see it" },
    { id: 47, level: "advanced", q: "What is a memory leak in the browser?", a: "A memory leak is memory you do not need but still hold a pointer to. Common causes are forgotten setInterval, global caches, detached DOM nodes, and closures holding huge data.\n\nThe garbage collector cannot free what you still reference. Long-lived pages show this most.\n\nIn the code: leaked is an array. tick pushes a new 1000-slot array every second. setInterval runs tick forever. The comment shows the fix: clearInterval and leaked = [].\n\nAlways save timer ids and clear them when the work should stop.", code: "let leaked = []; // a named box\nfunction tick() { // a reusable step\n  leaked.push(new Array(1000)); // grows forever\n}\nconst id = setInterval(tick, 1000); // run later\n// later: clearInterval(id); leaked = []; // release" },
    { id: 48, level: "advanced", q: "How does garbage collection work at a high level?", a: "If nothing can reach an object from roots such as the stack and globals, it can be collected. Mark-and-sweep marks the living objects, then sweeps the rest.\n\nNew short-lived objects are collected more often. You do not free memory by hand. Leaks are leftover references.\n\nIn the code: demo creates temp, returns temp.n, then ends. After demo() returns, temp can be collected. keep is still reachable, so { n: 2 } stays alive.\n\nDrop the last variable that points at large data when you are done with it.", code: "function demo() { // a reusable step\n  const temp = { n: 1 };    // only used here\n  return temp.n; // send this back\n}\ndemo();                     // temp can be collected after\nlet keep = { n: 2 };        // still reachable from keep" },
    { id: 49, level: "advanced", q: "What is the difference between == and Object.is?", a: "== converts types. === is almost “same value,” but NaN !== NaN, and +0 === -0. Object.is(NaN, NaN) is true, and Object.is(+0, -0) is false.\n\nLibraries use Object.is when they need exact same-value equality.\n\nIn the code: NaN === NaN is false. Object.is(NaN, NaN) is true. +0 === -0 is true. Object.is(+0, -0) is false. 1 == \"1\" is true because of conversion.\n\nUse === day to day. Use Object.is when NaN or signed zero must be distinct.", code: "console.log(NaN === NaN);         // false\nconsole.log(Object.is(NaN, NaN)); // true\nconsole.log(+0 === -0);           // true\nconsole.log(Object.is(+0, -0));   // false\nconsole.log(1 == \"1\");            // true (coercion)" },
    { id: 50, level: "advanced", q: "How do you implement bind?", a: "bind returns a new function. That function calls the original with a fixed this and any starter arguments. Later arguments get added on.\n\nA spec-perfect bind also still works with new. Writing a small bind shows you understand this.\n\nIn the code: myBind returns bound, which calls fn.apply(thisArg, preset plus later args). greet concatenates this.name and city. hi is greet bound to { name: \"Ada\" } with \"-\" preset.\n\nIf you forget apply, this will not be the object you locked in.", code: "function myBind(fn, thisArg, ...preset) { // a reusable step\n  return function bound(...later) { // send this back\n    return fn.apply(thisArg, [...preset, ...later]); // send this back\n  };\n}\nfunction greet(city) { return this.name + city; } // send this back\nconst hi = myBind(greet, { name: \"Ada\" }, \"-\"); // a named box" },
    { id: 51, level: "advanced", q: "How would you flatten a nested array?", a: "Flattening turns [1, [2, [3]]] into [1, 2, 3]. flat(Infinity) is the built-in. You can also recurse or use a stack so you do not blow the call stack.\n\nAsk how deep it can be and if holes matter.\n\nIn the code: nested.flat(Infinity) prints [1, 2, 3]. flatten uses reduce: if x is an array, flatten it, else concat x. Recursion walks every nested list.\n\nUse the built-in flat unless you must support a very old environment.", code: "const nested = [1, [2, [3]]]; // a named box\nconsole.log(nested.flat(Infinity)); // [1, 2, 3]\nfunction flatten(arr) { // a reusable step\n  return arr.reduce((out, x) => // send this back\n    out.concat(Array.isArray(x) ? flatten(x) : x), []); // shows the idea\n}" },
    { id: 52, level: "advanced", q: "How do you deep clone with cycles?", a: "A cycle is an object that points at itself, directly or through friends. Keep a WeakMap from original to clone. If you see the same object again, reuse the clone you already made.\n\nstructuredClone supports cycles. JSON does not, and it throws.\n\nIn the code: clone returns primitives as-is. If seen has obj, it returns the existing clone. Otherwise it makes [] or {}, records it in seen, then copies each key. That stops infinite loops.\n\nUse a seen map whenever you copy graphs, not only trees.", code: "function clone(obj, seen = new WeakMap()) { // a reusable step\n  if (obj === null || typeof obj !== \"object\") return obj; // send this back\n  if (seen.has(obj)) return seen.get(obj); // cycle\n  const out = Array.isArray(obj) ? [] : {}; // a named box\n  seen.set(obj, out); // shows the idea\n  for (const k of Object.keys(obj)) out[k] = clone(obj[k], seen); // repeat\n  return out; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 53, level: "advanced", q: "What is a WeakMap used for?", a: "A WeakMap is a Map that does not keep object keys alive. Keys must be objects, and they are held weakly. If nothing else points at the key, it can be garbage collected.\n\nGreat for private notes stuck onto objects. You cannot loop a WeakMap.\n\nIn the code: notes is a WeakMap. user is { name: \"Ada\" }. notes.set(user, \"vip\") stores metadata. get(user) reads it. If user is forgotten, the note can disappear too.\n\nUse WeakMap for metadata that should die with the object.", code: "const notes = new WeakMap(); // a named box\nconst user = { name: \"Ada\" }; // a named box\nnotes.set(user, \"vip\");     // metadata, weak key\nconsole.log(notes.get(user)); // print to see it\n// if user is forgotten, the note can disappear too" },
    { id: 54, level: "advanced", q: "What is the event loop difference in Node vs browser?", a: "Both the browser and Node run microtasks such as promises. Node has extra phases: timers, poll, check, close, and more. process.nextTick in Node runs even before other microtasks.\n\nThat matters if you mix timers and I/O on the server.\n\nIn the code: \"sync\" prints first. The promise logs \"promise\". setTimeout logs \"timer\". The comment notes that Node also has nextTick before most microtasks.\n\nDo not copy browser timing assumptions blindly onto Node.", code: "console.log(\"sync\"); // print to see it\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"promise\")); // a future result\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\"timer\"), 0); // run later\n// browser: sync, promise, timer\n// Node also has nextTick before most microtasks" },
    { id: 55, level: "advanced", q: "process.nextTick vs Promise vs setImmediate?", a: "In Node-style JavaScript, nextTick is extra urgent, even before promises. Promises are microtasks. setImmediate is a later macrotask in the check phase.\n\nIf nextTick keeps scheduling itself, it can starve other work.\n\nIn the code: a promise logs \"promise\". setTimeout logs \"timeout\". The nextTick line is commented. \"sync\" prints first. In Node, an enabled nextTick would print before the promise.\n\nOn the server, prefer promises unless you truly need nextTick.", code: "Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"promise\")); // a future result\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\"timeout\"), 0); // run later\n// nextTick would print before promise in Node:\n// process.nextTick(() => console.log(\"tick\"));\nconsole.log(\"sync\"); // print to see it" },
    { id: 56, level: "intermediate", q: "What is tail call optimization and does JS have it?", a: "A tail call is when you return a function call as the last step. Tail call optimization would reuse the stack so deep recursion does not crash.\n\nThe spec mentioned it, but most engines including V8 do not do it. Do not rely on it. Use a loop for deep work.\n\nIn the code: sum(n, acc) returns sum(n - 1, acc + n) as its last step. sum(5) still uses stack frames and prints 15. It is a tail call, but V8 will not reuse the frame.\n\nRewrite deep recursion as a loop when n can be large.", code: "function sum(n, acc = 0) { // a reusable step\n  if (n === 0) return acc; // send this back\n  return sum(n - 1, acc + n); // tail call (not optimized in V8)\n}\nconsole.log(sum(5));          // 15 — still uses stack frames" },
    { id: 57, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you stop a setInterval?", a: "setInterval gives you an id. clearInterval(id) stops it. Save the id in a variable. Also clear timers when a UI component goes away.\n\nOtherwise work runs forever in the background.\n\nIn the code: id is the interval that logs \"tick\" every 1000ms. clearInterval(id) stops it. Then \"stopped\" prints.\n\nA common mistake is calling setInterval and never storing the id, so you cannot stop it.", code: "const id = setInterval(() => { // run later\n  console.log(\"tick\"); // print to see it\n}, 1000); // shows the idea\n// later, stop the repeating timer\nclearInterval(id); // shows the idea\nconsole.log(\"stopped\"); // print to see it" },
    { id: 58, level: "beginner", q: "What is a callback?", a: "A callback is a function you give to someone else to call later. Events, timers, and older APIs use them.\n\nToo many nested callbacks become hard to read. Promises help flatten that mess.\n\nIn the code: load takes cb and calls it with \"done\" after 100ms. The caller passes (msg) => console.log(msg), so \"done\" prints later. setTimeout is what delays the callback.\n\nUse a callback when an API asks for one. Prefer promises for new async code.", code: "function load(cb) { // a reusable step\n  setTimeout(() => cb(\"done\"), 100); // call later\n}\nload((msg) => { // shows the idea\n  console.log(msg);                  // \"done\"\n}); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 59, level: "intermediate", q: "What is callback hell and how do you avoid it?", a: "Callback hell is deeply nested callbacks. Each “then do this” wraps another function, making a pyramid. Errors become messy. Reading top to bottom is hard.\n\nNamed functions, promises, or async/await flatten it.\n\nIn the code: the comment shows loadUser nested in loadPosts. The async version awaits fetchUser, then awaits fetchPosts, then returns posts. The flow is one column, not a pyramid.\n\nWhen you have three waits in a row, use async/await instead of nesting callbacks.", code: "// pyramid of doom (hard)\n// loadUser(id, (u) => loadPosts(u, (p) => ...))\nasync function load(id) { // can wait inside\n  const user = await fetchUser(id);  // flat\n  const posts = await fetchPosts(user.id); // pause until ready\n  return posts; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 60, level: "intermediate", q: "How does fetch differ from XMLHttpRequest?", a: "fetch returns a Promise and has a cleaner API than XMLHttpRequest. It does not reject on HTTP 404 or 500, only on network failure. You must check response.ok yourself.\n\nYou can abort with AbortController and read streams.\n\nIn the code: fetch(\"/api/user\") is awaited. If !res.ok, it throws. Then res.json() is awaited and logged. A 404 would hit the throw, not the json line.\n\nAlways check ok. fetch succeeding only means the network answered.", code: "const res = await fetch(\"/api/user\"); // ask the network\nif (!res.ok) {                 // 404 does not throw\n  throw new Error(\"not ok\"); // shows the idea\n}\nconst data = await res.json(); // pause until ready\nconsole.log(data); // print to see it" },
    { id: 61, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you cancel a fetch?", a: "Make an AbortController. Pass its signal into fetch. Call abort() when the user leaves or types a new search. Catch AbortError so it is not treated as a crash.\n\nThat stops old results from overwriting new ones.\n\nIn the code: ctrl is an AbortController. fetch uses signal: ctrl.signal. The catch ignores AbortError. ctrl.abort() stops the request.\n\nAbort the previous search fetch when the query changes.", code: "const ctrl = new AbortController(); // a named box\nfetch(\"/search?q=ada\", { signal: ctrl.signal })\n  .catch((err) => { // shows the idea\n    if (err.name === \"AbortError\") return; // user cancelled\n  }); // shows the idea\nctrl.abort(); // stop the request" },
    { id: 62, level: "beginner", q: "What is JSON?", a: "JSON is a text format for objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans, and null. It has no functions, comments, or undefined.\n\nJSON.parse turns text into data. JSON.stringify turns data into text. APIs use it all the time.\n\nIn the code: text is a JSON string. parse turns it into user with name Ada. stringify turns user back into text. user.name prints Ada.\n\nA common mistake is calling parse on an object that is already parsed.", code: "const text = '{\"name\":\"Ada\",\"age\":21}'; // a named box\nconst user = JSON.parse(text);     // text -> object\nconst back = JSON.stringify(user); // object -> text\nconsole.log(user.name); // print to see it" },
    { id: 63, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you sort numbers correctly?", a: "Array.sort without a function sorts as text. So 10 can come before 2 because \"1\" comes before \"2\". Pass (a, b) => a - b for numbers.\n\nAlways give a comparator for numbers. Use it for scores, prices, and ages.\n\nIn the code: nums is [10, 2, 1]. sort() without a function uses text order. sort((a, b) => a - b) is 1, 2, 10. sort((a, b) => b - a) is biggest first. slice() copies first so the original is not the only list you sort.\n\nNever sort numbers without a compare function.", code: "const nums = [10, 2, 1]; // a named box\nconsole.log(nums.slice().sort());           // text order\nconsole.log(nums.slice().sort((a, b) => a - b)); // 1, 2, 10\nconsole.log(nums.slice().sort((a, b) => b - a)); // biggest first" },
    { id: 64, level: "intermediate", q: "stable sort?", a: "A stable sort keeps equal items in their original order. Modern JavaScript Array.sort is stable. That lets you sort by name, then sort by grade, and names stay grouped.\n\nUse it for tables with two sort keys.\n\nIn the code: rows has Ada and Bob, both grade A. First sort is by name. Second sort is by grade. Equal grades keep the name order, so Ada still comes before Bob.\n\nIf you need two keys, sort by the secondary key first, then the primary key.", code: "const rows = [ // a named box\n  { name: \"Ada\", grade: \"A\" },\n  { name: \"Bob\", grade: \"A\" }\n];\nrows.sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name)); // shows the idea\nrows.sort((a, b) => a.grade.localeCompare(b.grade)); // shows the idea\n// equal grades keep the name order" },
    { id: 65, level: "advanced", q: "How would you implement Promise.all?", a: "To rebuild Promise.all, return a new promise. Store each result at the same index. When the last one succeeds, resolve the array. If any rejects, reject immediately.\n\nEmpty input should resolve to [].\n\nIn the code: all makes out and n = ps.length. If n is 0, it resolves []. forEach waits on each promise, writes out[i] = v, and resolves when n hits 0. reject is passed as the failure handler, so the first failure wins.\n\nKeep indexes. Do not push, or a slow first item could land last.", code: "function all(ps) {\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\n    const out = []; let n = ps.length; // results + how many left\n    if (!n) return resolve([]); // empty input\n    ps.forEach((p, i) => {\n      Promise.resolve(p).then((v) => {\n        out[i] = v; // keep the same order\n        if (--n === 0) resolve(out); // last success\n      }, reject); // first failure wins\n    });\n  });\n}" },
    { id: 66, level: "advanced", q: "How would you limit concurrent promises?", a: "Limiting concurrent promises means only N tasks run at once. Keep a queue and a running counter. When one finishes, start the next.\n\nThis protects APIs with rate limits, so you do not fire 10,000 fetches at once.\n\nIn the code: pool copies tasks into q. It starts n workers. Each worker shift()s a job and awaits it until q is empty. Promise.all waits for every worker.\n\nUse a pool when you map a large list to network calls.", code: "async function pool(tasks, n) { // can wait inside\n  const q = [...tasks]; // a named box\n  const workers = Array.from({ length: n }, async () => { // can wait inside\n    while (q.length) { // shows the idea\n      const job = q.shift();     // next task\n      await job();               // wait before taking another\n    }\n  }); // shows the idea\n  await Promise.all(workers); // a future result\n}" },
    { id: 67, level: "intermediate", q: "What is function composition?", a: "Function composition means the output of one function becomes the input of the next. compose(f, g)(x) means f(g(x)). pipe usually runs left to right.\n\nYou use this in data transforms.\n\nIn the code: double multiplies by 2. plus1 adds 1. pipe(3, plus1, double) runs plus1 first, then double, so (3+1)*2 is 8.\n\nWrite small functions, then pipe them, instead of one huge function.", code: "const double = (n) => n * 2; // a named box\nconst plus1 = (n) => n + 1; // a named box\nconst pipe = (x, ...fns) => fns.reduce((v, f) => f(v), x); // a named box\nconsole.log(pipe(3, plus1, double)); // (3+1)*2 = 8" },
    { id: 68, level: "beginner", q: "What is the arguments object?", a: "Non-arrow functions get a special arguments list-like object. It is not a real array, so map is awkward. Prefer rest parameters (...args), which is a real array.\n\nArrows do not have arguments.\n\nIn the code: old logs arguments[0]. newer(...args) can call args.map. newer(1, 2) passes two values into a real array.\n\nUse rest. Treat arguments as legacy.", code: "function old() { // a reusable step\n  console.log(arguments[0]); // list-like, not a real array\n}\nfunction newer(...args) { // a reusable step\n  console.log(args.map((x) => x)); // real array\n}\nnewer(1, 2); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 69, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a higher-order function?", a: "A higher-order function takes a function in, or returns a function, or both. map, filter, then, and addEventListener are everyday examples.\n\nJavaScript treats functions as values, so you can pass them around.\n\nIn the code: twice(fn) returns a function that calls fn twice. add1 adds 1. twice(add1)(3) is 5, because 3 becomes 4, then 5.\n\nIf you pass a function into map, you are already using a higher-order function.", code: "function twice(fn) { // a reusable step\n  return function (x) {     // returns a function\n    return fn(fn(x)); // send this back\n  };\n}\nconst add1 = (n) => n + 1; // a named box\nconsole.log(twice(add1)(3)); // 5" },
    { id: 70, level: "intermediate", q: "immutability in JS?", a: "Immutability means you return a new object or array instead of editing the old one. That helps React notice changes and makes undo easier.\n\nUse spread, map, and filter. For deep trees, libraries like Immer can help.\n\nIn the code: older is { ...user, age: 22 }, a new object. more is [...list, 3], a new array. user.age is still 21. The originals were not edited.\n\nCopy on write when other code might still be looking at the old value.", code: "const user = { name: \"Ada\", age: 21 }; // a named box\nconst older = { ...user, age: 22 }; // new object\nconst list = [1, 2]; // a named box\nconst more = [...list, 3];          // new array\nconsole.log(user.age);              // still 21" },
    { id: 71, level: "advanced", q: "What are typed arrays?", a: "Typed arrays such as Uint8Array sit on a raw ArrayBuffer of bytes. They are for files, images, crypto, and network packets.\n\nThey are not normal arrays: no mix of strings and objects. They help with performance and binary formats.\n\nIn the code: bytes is 4 slots, each 0–255. bytes[0] is 255. bytes[1] is 10. The log prints 255. buffer.byteLength is 4.\n\nUse a typed array when you are handling raw bytes, not when you need a general list.", code: "const bytes = new Uint8Array(4); // 4 slots, 0–255\nbytes[0] = 255; // shows the idea\nbytes[1] = 10; // shows the idea\nconsole.log(bytes[0]); // print to see it\nconsole.log(bytes.buffer.byteLength); // 4" },
    { id: 72, level: "intermediate", q: "What is CORS at a JS level?", a: "CORS is a browser safety rule. The browser blocks your JS from reading another origin unless that server allows it. The server sends Access-Control-Allow-Origin and related headers.\n\nSome requests send a preflight OPTIONS first. This is not a JavaScript syntax feature.\n\nIn the code: the page on app.com fetch()es api.other.com. json() only works if the other server allowed this origin. The comment explains the header check.\n\nYou cannot “fix CORS” only in frontend code. The other server must allow you.", code: "// your page is on https://app.com\nconst res = await fetch(\"https://api.other.com/data\");\n// browser only gives you the body if the other\n// server allowed this origin in its response headers\nconst data = await res.json(); // pause until ready\nconsole.log(data); // print to see it" },
    { id: 73, level: "intermediate", q: "localStorage vs sessionStorage vs cookies?", a: "localStorage stays until you clear it. sessionStorage lasts for that tab. Both are easy for JS to read, so they are risky if an XSS bug exists.\n\nCookies can be sent with requests and can be HttpOnly so JS cannot see them.\n\nIn the code: theme is saved in localStorage. step is saved in sessionStorage. getItem reads theme back. document.cookie sets a cookie string.\n\nDo not store passwords or tokens in localStorage if you can use an HttpOnly cookie instead.", code: "localStorage.setItem(\"theme\", \"dark\"); // stays\nsessionStorage.setItem(\"step\", \"2\");   // this tab\nconst theme = localStorage.getItem(\"theme\"); // a named box\ndocument.cookie = \"ok=1; path=/\";      // cookie string" },
    { id: 74, level: "beginner", q: "What does Array.isArray do and why not typeof?", a: "typeof [] is \"object\", which is useless for detecting arrays. Array.isArray([]) is the honest check. instanceof Array can fail for arrays from another iframe.\n\nUse Array.isArray in functions that accept mixed input.\n\nIn the code: typeof [] prints \"object\". Array.isArray([]) is true. Array.isArray({}) is false. sum returns 0 if x is not an array.\n\nNever use typeof to ask “is this a list?”", code: "console.log(typeof []);          // \"object\" — not helpful\nconsole.log(Array.isArray([]));  // true\nconsole.log(Array.isArray({}));  // false\nfunction sum(x) { // a reusable step\n  if (!Array.isArray(x)) return 0; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 75, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a Symbol?", a: "A Symbol is a unique primitive tag. Two symbols with the same description are still different. People use them as object keys that will not clash.\n\nWell-known symbols like Symbol.iterator teach JS how to loop you.\n\nIn the code: id and other are both Symbol(\"id\") but id === other is false. user uses [id]: 7 as a key. user[id] reads 7.\n\nUse a symbol key when you need a field that ordinary Object.keys will not treat as a normal name.", code: "const id = Symbol(\"id\"); // a named box\nconst other = Symbol(\"id\"); // a named box\nconsole.log(id === other);     // false: unique\nconst user = { [id]: 7 };      // hidden-ish key\nconsole.log(user[id]); // print to see it" },
    { id: 76, level: "intermediate", q: "enumerable vs own properties?", a: "Own means on this object, not the parent. Enumerable means show me in for...in and Object.keys. Object.keys is own plus enumerable strings.\n\ngetOwnPropertyNames also includes non-enumerable names. That matters when you copy objects or hide fields.\n\nIn the code: Object.keys(user) is [\"name\"]. \"toString\" in user is true because it is inherited. Object.hasOwn(user, \"toString\") is false.\n\nUse hasOwn when you mean “this object itself has this key.”", code: "const user = { name: \"Ada\" }; // a named box\nconsole.log(Object.keys(user));              // [\"name\"]\nconsole.log(\"toString\" in user);             // true, inherited\nconsole.log(Object.hasOwn(user, \"toString\")); // false" },
    { id: 77, level: "advanced", q: "defineProperty vs a normal assignment?", a: "user.age = 1 uses default flags: usually writable and enumerable. Object.defineProperty can turn those flags off. You can make a field read-only or invisible to Object.keys.\n\nLibraries use this for constants on objects.\n\nIn the code: user starts empty. id is defined with value 1, writable false, enumerable false. console.log(user.id) prints 1. Object.keys would skip id.\n\nIf a field will not change, defineProperty can lock it. Normal assignment cannot.", code: "const user = {}; // a named box\nObject.defineProperty(user, \"id\", {\n  value: 1,\n  writable: false,     // cannot change\n  enumerable: false    // hidden from Object.keys\n}); // shows the idea\nconsole.log(user.id); // print to see it" },
    { id: 78, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a Proxy?", a: "A Proxy is a wrapper that can watch get, set, and more. You can validate, log, or make defaults. Vue’s reactivity used this idea.\n\nThere is a speed cost.\n\nIn the code: proxy wraps user. The get trap logs \"read\" and the key, then returns obj[key]. proxy.name prints Ada after logging read name.\n\nUse a proxy when you must intercept every read or write. Do not wrap everything by habit.", code: "const user = { name: \"Ada\" }; // a named box\nconst proxy = new Proxy(user, { // a named box\n  get(obj, key) {\n    console.log(\"read\", key);  // watch reads\n    return obj[key]; // send this back\n  }\n}); // shows the idea\nconsole.log(proxy.name); // print to see it" },
    { id: 79, level: "intermediate", q: "What is Reflect?", a: "Reflect has functions that match language operations: get, set, apply. Inside a Proxy, you often call Reflect so default behavior stays correct.\n\nIt returns booleans instead of throwing in some cases.\n\nIn the code: the get trap returns Reflect.get(obj, key, rec). proxy.name still prints Ada, but through the default get. rec is the receiver, which keeps getters working.\n\nIf you write a proxy trap, call Reflect unless you are fully replacing the operation.", code: "const user = { name: \"Ada\" }; // a named box\nconst proxy = new Proxy(user, { // a named box\n  get(obj, key, rec) {\n    return Reflect.get(obj, key, rec); // default get\n  }\n}); // shows the idea\nconsole.log(proxy.name); // print to see it" },
    { id: 80, level: "beginner", q: "How do you convert a string to a number?", a: "Number(str), parseInt(str, 10), parseFloat, or unary + can turn text into a number. Number(\"\") is 0. parseInt(\"10px\", 10) is 10.\n\nAlways pass radix 10 to parseInt so you do not get octal surprises. Form inputs are always text.\n\nIn the code: Number(\"21\") is 21. parseInt(\"10px\", 10) is 10. parseFloat(\"3.5kg\") is 3.5. Number(\"\") is 0.\n\nCheck Number.isFinite after converting, because bad text becomes NaN.", code: "console.log(Number(\"21\"));         // 21\nconsole.log(parseInt(\"10px\", 10)); // 10 (radix 10)\nconsole.log(parseFloat(\"3.5kg\"));  // 3.5\nconsole.log(Number(\"\"));           // 0" },
    { id: 81, level: "beginner", q: "What is type coercion?", a: "Type coercion is when JavaScript secretly converts types. Operators like + and == may change a value to another type. If either side of + is a string, + often becomes glue for text.\n\nMinus, times, and divide try to make numbers. Do not rely on magic conversions in real APIs.\n\nIn the code: 1 + \"2\" is \"12\". 1 - \"2\" is -1. \"5\" * 2 is 10. true + 1 is 2. The same looking values behave differently by operator.\n\nConvert types yourself with Number or String so the result is obvious.", code: "console.log(1 + \"2\");   // \"12\" text glue\nconsole.log(1 - \"2\");   // -1  number math\nconsole.log(\"5\" * 2);   // 10\nconsole.log(true + 1);  // 2" },
    { id: 82, level: "advanced", q: "Why can 0.1 + 0.2 !== 0.3?", a: "Computers store many decimals as approximations. 0.1 and 0.2 cannot be exact in binary, so the sum is a tiny bit off. Compare with a small allowed error, or use integers for money (cents).\n\nOtherwise a price check can fail.\n\nIn the code: 0.1 + 0.2 prints 0.30000000000000004. === 0.3 is false. close uses Math.abs(...) < 1e-10, which is true enough.\n\nNever compare money with === on decimals. Store cents as integers.", code: "console.log(0.1 + 0.2);              // 0.30000000000000004\nconsole.log(0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3);      // false\nconst close = Math.abs(0.1 + 0.2 - 0.3) < 1e-10; // a named box\nconsole.log(close);                  // true enough" },
    { id: 83, level: "intermediate", q: "What is BigInt?", a: "BigInt can be bigger than the safe Number limit. Write 10n or BigInt(\"10\"). You cannot mix BigInt and Number in math.\n\nJSON does not support it by default.\n\nIn the code: big is 9007199254740993n. big + 1n works. big + 1 is commented because mixing throws. BigInt(\"10\") === 10n is true.\n\nUse BigInt for huge integers. Use Number for ordinary math and most UI.", code: "const big = 9007199254740993n; // n means BigInt\nconsole.log(big + 1n); // print to see it\n// console.log(big + 1);       // error: cannot mix\nconsole.log(BigInt(\"10\") === 10n); // print to see it" },
    { id: 84, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you make an object immutable?", a: "const only locks the variable name, not the object insides. Object.freeze is shallow: nested objects can still change. A deep freeze walks the tree.\n\nIn apps, copying on write is often more practical.\n\nIn the code: freeze(user) locks the top layer. user.name = \"Bob\" is commented. user.stats.xp = 2 still works, and the log prints 2. stats was not frozen.\n\nFreeze nested objects too, or copy them, if you need a real lock.", code: "const user = { name: \"Ada\", stats: { xp: 1 } }; // a named box\nObject.freeze(user); // shows the idea\n// user.name = \"Bob\";          // ignored or error in strict\nuser.stats.xp = 2;             // still changes (shallow)\nconsole.log(user.stats.xp); // print to see it" },
    { id: 85, level: "advanced", q: "What is a realm / iframe issue with instanceof?", a: "Each iframe has its own Array constructor. An array from the other frame is not instanceof this page’s Array. Array.isArray still works across those worlds.\n\nThis shows up in apps with previews or widgets in iframes.\n\nIn the code: list instanceof Array is true here. Array.isArray(list) is true anywhere. The comment notes that an array from another iframe can fail instanceof.\n\nUse Array.isArray, not instanceof Array, when data might cross frames.", code: "const list = []; // a named box\nconsole.log(list instanceof Array); // true here\nconsole.log(Array.isArray(list));   // true anywhere\n// list from another iframe can fail instanceof Array" },
    { id: 86, level: "intermediate", q: "How do modules live-bind imports?", a: "ES imports are live: if the exporter changes the binding, you see the new value. You did not get a frozen photocopy of the number.\n\nCommonJS often copies at require time unless you mutate the exports object. Circular modules and counters depend on this.\n\nIn the code: counter.js exports n and bump. app.js imports both, calls bump(), then logs n. n is 1 because it is a live binding, not a stale copy.\n\nDo not assign to an imported name. Call a function that updates it on the exporter side.", code: "// counter.js\nexport let n = 0; // share this name\nexport function bump() { n = n + 1; } // share this name\n// app.js\nimport { n, bump } from \"./counter.js\"; // bring in a name\nbump(); // shows the idea\nconsole.log(n); // 1 — live binding, not a stale copy" },
    { id: 87, level: "beginner", q: "What is the difference between slice and splice?", a: "slice copies a range and does not change the original. splice changes the original: it can delete and insert. slice returns the copy. splice returns the removed items.\n\nMutating by accident is a common bug.\n\nIn the code: copy is list.slice(0, 2), so [\"a\", \"b\"], and list is unchanged. splice(1, 1) removes \"b\" from list. The log shows copy, gone, and the new list.\n\nUse slice to copy. Use splice only when you mean to edit the original array.", code: "const list = [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]; // a named box\nconst copy = list.slice(0, 2);  // [\"a\", \"b\"], list unchanged\nconst gone = list.splice(1, 1); // removes \"b\" from list\nconsole.log(copy, gone, list); // print to see it" },
    { id: 88, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you remove duplicates from an array of objects?", a: "Objects are unique by identity, so Set will not merge two {id:1} copies. Use a Map or Set keyed by a field like id. If uniqueness is “all fields equal,” you need a careful hash.\n\nAsk what unique means. Two objects can look the same and still be different.\n\nIn the code: rows has two { id: 1 } and one { id: 2 }. The Map stores by row.id, so the last one with that id wins. unique.length is 2.\n\nKey by id, not by the object itself, when records can be duplicates.", code: "const rows = [{ id: 1 }, { id: 1 }, { id: 2 }]; // a named box\nconst map = new Map(); // a named box\nfor (const row of rows) { // repeat\n  map.set(row.id, row);          // last one with that id wins\n}\nconst unique = [...map.values()]; // make a new list\nconsole.log(unique.length);      // 2" },
    { id: 89, level: "advanced", q: "How would you implement debounce?", a: "To write debounce, remember a timeout id. On each call, cancel the last timeout and start a new one. When calls stop, the function finally runs.\n\nYou can add a cancel method. Search-as-you-type uses this.\n\nIn the code: wrapped clears id, then sets a new timeout to call fn. wrapped.cancel clears the timeout. The function returns wrapped.\n\nClear the timeout on unmount if this debounce lives in a UI component.", code: "function debounce(fn, ms) { // a reusable step\n  let id; // a named box\n  const wrapped = (...args) => { // a named box\n    clearTimeout(id); // shows the idea\n    id = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms); // run later\n  };\n  wrapped.cancel = () => clearTimeout(id); // shows the idea\n  return wrapped; // send this back\n}" },
    { id: 90, level: "advanced", q: "How would you implement EventEmitter?", a: "An EventEmitter stores a Map from event name to a set of listeners. on adds, off removes, emit calls them all. once wraps a listener that removes itself.\n\nCopy the list before emit so adding during emit is safe.\n\nIn the code: createBus makes map. on adds fn to a Set for that event. emit copies the set to an array and calls each fn with data.\n\nCopy the listeners. If you iterate the live Set, a listener can add another listener and surprise you.", code: "function createBus() { // a reusable step\n  const map = new Map(); // a named box\n  return { // send this back\n    on(ev, fn) {\n      if (!map.has(ev)) map.set(ev, new Set()); // pick a path\n      map.get(ev).add(fn); // shows the idea\n    },\n    emit(ev, data) {\n      [...(map.get(ev) || [])].forEach((fn) => fn(data)); // shows the idea\n    }\n  };\n}" },
    { id: 91, level: "intermediate", q: "What is requestAnimationFrame?", a: "requestAnimationFrame schedules work right before the next paint. That matches the screen refresh better than setInterval. Cancel with cancelAnimationFrame.\n\nUse it for smooth animations.\n\nIn the code: frame schedules itself again with requestAnimationFrame. id is saved. The last comment shows cancelAnimationFrame(id). time is passed by the browser.\n\nDo not use setInterval(16) for animation. Use requestAnimationFrame.", code: "let id; // a named box\nfunction frame(time) { // a reusable step\n  // move a box a little, then ask for the next frame\n  id = requestAnimationFrame(frame); // shows the idea\n}\nid = requestAnimationFrame(frame); // shows the idea\n// later: cancelAnimationFrame(id);" },
    { id: 92, level: "intermediate", q: "layout thrashing?", a: "Layout thrashing is mixing layout reads and writes so the browser recalculates over and over. Reading offsetHeight forces layout now. If you write styles, then read, then write, it repeats.\n\nBatch all reads, then all writes. Scroll and drag code often hits this.\n\nIn the code: heights collects every offsetHeight first. Then each element’s style.height is set. Reads are in one loop, writes in the next.\n\nNever read layout in the same loop that writes styles.", code: "const els = document.querySelectorAll(\".box\"); // a named box\nconst heights = []; // a named box\nels.forEach((el) => heights.push(el.offsetHeight)); // all reads\nels.forEach((el, i) => { // shows the idea\n  el.style.height = heights[i] + 10 + \"px\";         // then writes\n}); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 93, level: "beginner", q: "innerHTML vs textContent vs innerText?", a: "innerHTML parses HTML, which is dangerous with user input. textContent sets plain text and is usually what you want. innerText cares about CSS visibility and is slower.\n\nUse textContent so user text cannot become tags.\n\nIn the code: textContent = \"<b>hi</b>\" shows the tags as text. innerHTML = \"<b>hi</b>\" makes bold text. innerText is then logged.\n\nIf the string came from a user, use textContent, not innerHTML.", code: "const el = document.querySelector(\"#msg\"); // a named box\nel.textContent = \"<b>hi</b>\"; // shows the tags as text\nel.innerHTML = \"<b>hi</b>\";   // makes bold (unsafe if user data)\nconsole.log(el.innerText); // print to see it" },
    { id: 94, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you prevent XSS in the DOM?", a: "XSS means an attacker injects script through HTML. Never assign untrusted strings to innerHTML. Use textContent for user text.\n\nIf you must render HTML, sanitize it first. One bad comment can steal cookies.\n\nIn the code: showComment sets el.textContent = userText. The innerHTML line is commented as dangerous if userText has a script tag.\n\nTreat every user string as unsafe until it is plain text.", code: "function showComment(el, userText) { // a reusable step\n  el.textContent = userText; // always plain text\n  // el.innerHTML = userText; // dangerous if userText has <script>\n}" },
    { id: 95, level: "advanced", q: "What is a service worker at a high level?", a: "A service worker is a background script that can intercept fetch and cache files. That helps offline apps. It cannot touch the DOM.\n\nIt runs on a separate thread with async APIs.\n\nIn the code: the fetch listener calls event.respondWith. caches.match looks for a stored response. If cached exists, it is used; otherwise fetch goes to the network.\n\nRegister a service worker only if you need offline or custom caching. It can be hard to debug.", code: "// inside a service worker file\nself.addEventListener(\"fetch\", (event) => { // ask the network\n  event.respondWith(\n    caches.match(event.request).then((cached) => { // shows the idea\n      return cached || fetch(event.request); // cache or network\n    })\n  );\n}); // shows the idea" },
    { id: 96, level: "intermediate", q: "Why might a for loop with var and setTimeout print 3 3 3?", a: "var is one variable for the whole function. The loop finishes with i === 3 before the timeouts run. All three callbacks read that same i.\n\nlet creates a fresh i per round. That is the classic closure plus var bug.\n\nIn the code: the var loop logs 3 3 3. The let loop logs 0 1 2. Both use setTimeout(..., 0).\n\nUse let in for loops when the body is delayed.", code: "for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) { // repeat\n  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0); // 3 3 3\n}\nfor (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) { // repeat\n  setTimeout(() => console.log(j), 0); // 0 1 2\n}" },
    { id: 97, level: "advanced", q: "What is the difference between shadowing and a closure bug?", a: "Shadowing is an inner name hiding an outer name. A classic closure bug is capturing a changing var that later has a new value. They both involve nested functions, but the fix is different.\n\nDraw the boxes of scope. Shadowing is two names. The closure bug is one name that changes over time.\n\nIn the code: inner const x = 2 hides the outer x = 1, so inner returns 2. later just calls fn(). The comment says a closure bug is sharing one var across delayed functions, not hiding a name.\n\nIf a delayed function prints the last loop value, that is the closure bug, not shadowing.", code: "const x = 1; // a named box\nfunction inner() { // a reusable step\n  const x = 2;           // shadowing: inner x hides outer x\n  return x; // send this back\n}\nfunction later(fn) { fn(); } // a reusable step\n// closure bug is sharing one var across delayed fns, not hiding a name" },
    { id: 98, level: "intermediate", q: "How do you check if a key exists on an object?", a: "key in obj also looks at the parent prototype. Object.hasOwn(obj, key) is own-only. obj[key] !== undefined misses keys whose value is actually undefined.\n\nUse hasOwn for optional fields that might be undefined.\n\nIn the code: \"name\" in user is true. hasOwn(user, \"age\") is true even though age is undefined. user.age !== undefined is false, so that test misses the key.\n\nDo not use !== undefined to ask whether a key exists.", code: "const user = { name: \"Ada\", age: undefined }; // a named box\nconsole.log(\"name\" in user);              // true\nconsole.log(Object.hasOwn(user, \"age\"));  // true, even if undefined\nconsole.log(user.age !== undefined);      // false — misses the key" },
    { id: 99, level: "advanced", q: "What are tagged templates used for?", a: "A tagged template is fn`string ${value}`. The function receives the string chunks and the inserted values separately. Libraries use this for CSS-in-JS, GraphQL, and sanitizing HTML.\n\nIt is safer than gluing raw strings when values are untrusted.\n\nIn the code: tag logs parts, then values, then returns parts[0] + values[0]. name is \"Ada\". tag`Hello ${name}` calls tag with those chunks.\n\nWrite a tag function when you need to treat inserted values differently from the surrounding text.", code: "function tag(parts, ...values) { // a reusable step\n  console.log(parts);     // string chunks\n  console.log(values);    // inserted values\n  return parts[0] + values[0]; // send this back\n}\nconst name = \"Ada\"; // a named box\ntag`Hello ${name}`; // shows the idea" },
    { id: 100, level: "advanced", q: "How do you reason about time complexity of array methods?", a: "map, filter, and forEach walk the whole list: about O(n), meaning work grows with the length. Nested loops over the same data can become O(n²). sort is about O(n log n).\n\nindexOf is O(n). A Set is better for many lookups, about O(1) on average. Huge lists can freeze the page.\n\nIn the code: map is O(n). sort is O(n log n). seen is a Set of nums. seen.has(1) is a fast lookup.\n\nIf you look up many times, build a Set once. Do not call indexOf inside a loop over the same list.", code: "const nums = [3, 1, 2]; // a named box\nnums.map((n) => n * 2);          // O(n)\nnums.sort((a, b) => a - b);      // O(n log n)\nconst seen = new Set(nums); // a named box\nconsole.log(seen.has(1));        // O(1) average lookup" }
  ]
};
